## 16. What I Learned From the Debugging

The biggest lesson from this problem was not the suffix array.

It was the distinction between **feasibility** and **greedy choice**.

Initially I was thinking:

> "Can this index be the mismatch?"

But that isn't enough.

The real question is:

> **"Can this index be the next element of the lexicographically smallest valid sequence?"**

Those are different questions.

An index can be a valid mismatch position, but choosing it may be worse than first taking an exact match at the same or earlier position.

The counterexample:

```text
word1 = "ghhgghhhhhh"
word2 = "gg"
```

made that distinction obvious.

The final strategy became:

```text
Exact match
    ↓
take it immediately

Mismatch
    ↓
use replacement only if necessary
and only if the suffix can finish

Otherwise
    ↓
skip
```

> **When optimizing a lexicographic sequence, don't greedily optimize the "special operation." Greedily optimize the sequence itself.**
> {: .prompt-tip }

---

## 17. Complexity

We scan `word1` a constant number of times.

The suffix preprocessing takes:

$$
O(n)
$$

The final greedy scan takes:

$$
O(n)
$$

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{O(n)}
$$

time complexity.

The suffix array requires:

$$
\boxed{O(n)}
$$

additional space.

---

## 18. Final Takeaway

The solution did not start with the final greedy algorithm.

It evolved through several questions:

```text
Can I brute force all subsequences?
        ↓
No.

What does a valid subsequence look like?
        ↓
Exact prefix + at most one mismatch + exact suffix.

Can I characterize the prefix?
        ↓
Build prefix information.

Can I characterize the suffix?
        ↓
Build suffix information.

Can I find a feasible mismatch position?
        ↓
Yes.

Is the earliest feasible mismatch necessarily optimal?
        ↓
No.

Counterexample:
"ghhgghhhhhh", "gg"
        ↓
[0,3] vs [0,1]

What actually needs to be greedy?
        ↓
The answer indices themselves.

Final strategy:
exact match first,
otherwise use the one replacement if the suffix can finish.
```

That debugging path is the important part.

The final code is relatively short, but the real work was discovering **what exactly needed to be greedy**.
